This study investigates the effectiveness of firebreaks in containing spreading forest fires. 
The research question is: under the condition of equal total area, 
does a densely arranged firebreak perform better than a sparsely arranged one in reducing fire spread?
We hypothesize that densely arranged firebreaks form more continuous barriers, 
thereby reducing both burned area and fire propagation distance.
The model represents the forest as a two-dimensional square grid,
where each cell can be in one of three states: flammable forest, burning, or non-flammable.
The non-flammable areas include burned regions and firebreaks. 
Burning cells can ignite adjacent forest cells before transitioning to the non-flammable state. 
The model uses synchronous updating and incorporates wind direction, allowing flames to spread more easily downwind.
Experiments compare three scenarios—no firebreak, sparse firebreak, 
and dense firebreak—and are run multiple times under different wind conditions. 
Evaluation metrics include remaining forest area at fixed time steps, maximum spread distance, 
cumulative burn ratio, and probability of fire reaching the map boundary. 
Finally, the effects of different firebreak configurations are analyzed.
